.TH PROLOG\-NOTEBOOK 1 "2026-09-06" "prolog\-notebook 0.10.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
prolog\-notebook \- Jupyter\-style notebooks for Prolog
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prolog\-notebook
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIfile(s)\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Notebooks for Prolog that a reader can run: a chapter is a markdown file with
prose and Prolog in it, and the pages this builds carry SWI\-Prolog compiled to
WebAssembly, so the engine is in the page rather than on a server.
.PP
The operand is a filter. Name chapters and a command acts on those; name none
and it acts on the whole book \(em the chapters prolog\-notebook\-index.md lists.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B guide [<options>]
the tour: a project, a book, and the loop
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pager
print it rather than opening a pager
.RE
.TP
.B new [<options>] <file(s)>
start a chapter, wired up and in the book
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-title <text>
the chapter's heading (default: from the filename)
.RE
.TP
.B view [<options>] [<file(s)>]
read it in a browser, cells and all
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-port <n>
what it listens on (default 8777)
.TP
.B \-\-no\-open
print the URL instead of opening a browser
.TP
.B \-\-built
serve the site as built, rather than your sources as they are
.RE
.TP
.B build [<options>] [<file(s)>]
write the site you can host or send
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-out <dir>
where the site is (default: the nearest prolog\-notebook\-site)
.TP
.B \-\-root
